Reference: Rhine Falls - Rhine Falls: The Rhine Falls (German: Rheinfall, [ˈʁaɪ̯nˌfal] (listen), singular) is a waterfall located in Switzerland and the most powerful waterfall in Europe. The falls are located on the High Rhine on the border between the cantons of Schaffhausen (SH) and Zürich (ZH), between the municipalities of Neuhausen am Rheinfall (SH) and Laufen-Uhwiesen/Dachsen (ZH), next to the town of Schaffhausen in northern Switzerland.
They are 150 metres (490 ft) wide and 23 metres (75 ft) high. In the winter months, the average water flow is 250 m³/s (8,800 cu ft/s), while in the summer, the average water flow is 600 m³/s (21,000 cu ft/s). The highest flow ever measured was more than 1250m³/s in 1999, previous to that the record was 1,250 cubic metres per second (44,000 cu ft/s) in 1965; 
